      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To determine if body surface mapping (BSM) is better than the standard 12 lead ECG in the diagnosis of acute myocardial infarction amongst emergency department patients."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "A University affiliated inner-city emergency department."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PARTICIPANTS",
          "content": "People presenting to an emergency department with symptoms compatible with myocardial ischaemia/infarction."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES",
          "content": "Myocardial infarction as defined by either standard 12 lead ECG changes with associated cardiac marker rise, Troponin T >0.1 microg/ml at > 12 h or autopsy/surgical findings of fresh macroscopic infarction."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "BSM had an overall sensitivity of 47.1% versus 40% for the 12 lead ECG (P < 0.001). Specificity for the BSM was 85.6% versus 93.7% for the 12 lead ECG (P < 0.001). These findings were consistent for low/moderate and high risk subgroups. Bayesian analysis demonstrates that indiscriminate use of BSM would result in a clinically important overdiagnosis of myocardial infarction amongst emergency department patients."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "BSM has a higher sensitivity, but a lower specificity for the diagnosis of myocardial infarction."
        }
      ]},
